      "text": "{\"front\": \"KARYYXVFMFYY\", \"middle\": \"QWGZDBHSXFEC\", \"end\": \"TRFSYYLLEMQM\", \"closing_stock\": 72}\n\n### Comprehensive Warehouse Audit Procedure for Special SKU Reconciliation\n\n**1. Introduction and Objective**\n\nThe purpose of this audit procedure is to establish a rigorous, repeatable framework for verifying the physical and financial integrity of high-value or high-risk inventory, specifically focusing on Special SKUs like \"ZX.\" Unlike standard bulk commodities, Special SKUs often require granular tracking due to their unique handling requirements, higher unit cost, or susceptibility to damage. This procedure outlines the end-to-end process for receiving, counting, reconciling discrepancies, documenting damage, and obtaining final sign-off. The goal is to ensure that the ledger accurately reflects physical reality, minimizing financial loss and operational inefficiency.\n\n**2. Phase 1: Receiving and Intake Verification**\n\nThe audit trail begins at the point of entry. For Special SKU ZX, the receiving process must be distinct from general stock to prevent commingling errors.\n\n*   **Step 1.1: Pre-Arrival Notification.** Before the truck arrives, the warehouse manager must review the Purchase Order (PO) and the Advance Shipping Notice (ASN). The auditor verifies that the expected quantity matches the PO. For example, if the ledger indicates an opening stock of 133 units and a receipt of 16 units is expected, the system should flag this incoming shipment for priority inspection.\n*   **Step 1.2: Physical Inspection upon Arrival.** Upon arrival, the receiving clerk must inspect the outer packaging for visible damage. Any crushed boxes, water stains, or broken seals must be photographed and noted on the Bill of Lading (BOL) before the driver signs. This creates a legal record of the",
